1: # OS49 cap-data loops: the mixed cusp and its actual endpoint
2: 
3: Complete working proof submitted for P3K.44. Fix the actual OS49 source,
4: \(M=\mathcal O(G-O)\), suspension translation \(-3G\), selected
5: \(I_2\) at \(q=1/t=0\), the good IV-star cap of order
6: \(m=3k\), \(k>0\), norm \(n=\pm1\), its finite delta
7: character \(d\), and an actual periodic index-one fan. The scalar
8: is \(\lambda\); P3K.37 constructs its shorter parameter
9: \(\lambda=w^k\). We vary the actual affine source maps, line
10: units, and transported annular maps with their specified linear
11: source/lattice marking. Source-moduli, moving cap sites, changing the
12: discrete norm/order, and arbitrary nontranslation endpoints are not
13: included. In particular no whole-zero I1 cusp group is imported.
14: 
15: The global extending translation group is the physical
16: \(\mathbb C^*\). A closed fixed-scalar family in this marked
17: cap-data scope has zero return. A closed loop with scalar winding
18: \(\ell\) has \(\ell=Nk\), and its physical return is the
19: \(N\)-th power of P3K.37's actual shorter OS49 return. For the
20: same transported fan its class is
21: \(Nk(S-1)(S-3)/4\pmod{28}\). The exact mixed-cusp maps and
22: valuation proof follow.
23: 
24: ## OC.1. The actual local groups and the Lie extension
25: 
26: Away from the selected I2 the source line is the actual degree-zero
27: line on every reduced fibre, and the suspension lift is invertible.
28: On the good IV-star root use the corrected root line of F26/F29 and
29: K37, with its original finite character. Its central Picard point
30: \(G'\) has order three, and the effective deck has order three.
31: The suspension point \(Q'=3G'\) is zero on that central fibre.
32: For any actual affine source root \(a=t_S D\) the exact equation is
33: \[
34:        a^m=t_{-nQ'},\qquad
35:        \sum_{j=0}^{m-1}D^jS=-nQ'.                         \tag{OC.1}
36: \]
37: The branch \(R\) with \(mR=Q'\), \(R(0)=O\), is
38: well defined and deck invariant. Put \(Z=S+nR\). On the central
39: elliptic curve \(1-D_0\) is a surjective isogeny. Choose a local
40: inverse image of \(Z_0\), extend it to a section, and subtract
41: its \(1-D\) image. Write the chosen extending central inverse-image section as
42: \(\widetilde T\), and put
43: \(Z_1=Z-(1-D)\widetilde T\). This remainder has central value zero
44: and cyclic norm zero. In that zero-central section group the analytic
45: inverse of multiplication by \(m\) gives the literal solution
46: \[
47:  T=\widetilde T-[m]^{-1}\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}jD^jZ_1,
48:                  \qquad (1-D)T=Z.                        \tag{OC.2}
49: \] Substitution
50: gives \(t_T(t_{-nR}D)t_T^{-1}=a\). This is the good-root
51: construction of F44, now on this actual OS49 root. Its central kernel
52: of order three is retained; no inverse image is silently identified
53: with another.
54: 
55: There is an extending line lift of \(t_T\). On the smooth
56: generic elliptic fibre the Hom line is trivial by its constant
57: degree-zero factors of automorphy. Proper direct-image evaluation
58: extends it over the good root; any vertical divisor is a multiple of
59: the reduced fibre, and division by the corresponding base power gives
60: an invertible line map. At the retained type-II fibre the same
61: argument uses its one reduced component. On a generic flat-line
62: model every translation lift is \((z,V)\mapsto(z+t,cV)\),
63: because the ratio of any two such lifts is a holomorphic function on
64: a compact elliptic curve and is constant. Thus these lifts commute
65: with each other and with the actual suspension. Equality extends
66: over the root and retained fibre. The remaining line-unit norm is
67: the exact cyclic product; its central \(m\)-th root of unity is
68: retained and its zero-central logarithm is removed by the finite sum
69: in F44. This supplies analytic local conjugacies, including line and
70: annular maps, on a small parameter disc.
71: 
72: Let \(A\) be the sheaf of extending fibre translations of the
73: actual compactified OS49 suspension and \(V\) its Lie sheaf.
74: On the orbifold base with the order-\(m\) root there is the exact
75: vector-bundle sequence
76: \[
77:  0\longrightarrow\mathcal O\delta\longrightarrow V
78:           \longrightarrow L\longrightarrow0.             \tag{OC.3}
79: \]
80: At the good IV-star root the actual Weierstrass substitution is
81: \(t=z^{3k}\), \(x=z^{4k}X\), \(y=z^{6k}Y\). Hence the
82: original relative dualizing differential is
83: \(z^{-2k}dX/(2Y)\), and the original source translation vector
84: is \(z^{2k}\) times the root vector. The invariant holomorphic
85: Lie sections are exactly these original source sections, since
86: \(0\le2k<3k\). At the retained II and selected I2 the
87: logarithmic source vector is the original dualizing vector. At I2
88: this assertion follows directly from the unit torus maps in OC.2
89: below, with source coordinate \(Z\) and line coordinate \(V\).
90: Thus the coarse extension of \(L\) is the source
91: \(\mathcal O(-1)\), and that of the physical delta line is
92: \(\mathcal O\). Finite averaging is exact for this complex
93: vector sheaf, so
94: \[
95:                          H^1(B,V)=0.                      \tag{OC.4}
96: \]
97: 
98: As in the actual atlas construction of K41, the exponential sequence
99: \(0\to H\to V\to A\to0\) is exact on the full root
100: atlas and groupoid. This does not assert an invariant logarithm for
101: every fixed torsion point of the coarse root disc. The finite-group
102: lattice discrepancies remain part of the total Cech two-cocycle.
103: They are locally constant under parameter variation. Subtracting the
104: reference parameter gives a Lie one-cocycle, and (OC.4), solved by
105: the analytic Laurent/Cauchy splitting and finite vector averaging,
106: gives an actual interval comparison. Its endpoint is a global
107: extending translation. We compute that group using this cusp's
108: actual period matrix.
109: 
110: ## OC.2. The mixed cusp, with both integer reindexings
111: 
112: Use K37's actual Tate line coordinates. The source period \(h\),
113: suspension \(B_\lambda\), and rational forward lift \(U\)
114: of \(+G\) have the form
115: \[
116:  \begin{aligned}
117:  h:(Z,V)&\longmapsto(q^2H(q)Z,qP(q)V),\\
118:  B_\lambda:(Z,V)&\longmapsto(q^{-3}J(q)Z,
119:                                       q^{-1}\lambda C(q)V),\\
120:  U:(Z,V)&\longmapsto(qg(q)Z,q^{e_c}u(q)V),
121:  \end{aligned}                                             \tag{OC.5}
122: \]
123: where all displayed capital/lowercase coefficient functions are
124: holomorphic units. K37 chooses \(e_c=0\); we retain an arbitrary
125: integral whole-fibre change of that rational lift. Its exact graph is
126: \(h_j=\lceil j/2\rceil\), and the two \(+G\) defects are
127: \(-1,0\), so \(U\)'s actual pole divisor is \(-C_0\)
128: in the chosen normalization. The maps and source/line characters
129: in (OC.5) are those graph maps; no whole-fibre replacement of this
130: component divisor is made.
131: 
132: The valuation vectors of the first two maps are
133: \[
134:                    h=(2,1),\qquad B=(-3,-1),\qquad
135:              \det\begin{pmatrix}2&-3\\1&-1\end{pmatrix}=1.
136:                                                                \tag{OC.6}
137: \]
138: They generate the entire height-one component lattice of the
139: periodic toric covering. Consider an extending translation with
140: identity linear lattice part. It sends a central component and its
141: open torus to another such component and open torus: these are
142: components of the central fibre minus its intersection strata, which
143: an automorphism of the fibration preserves. Choose that image
144: component in the toric cover and reindex by its actual periods. On
145: the open torus the reindexed map is
146: \[
147:                     (Z,V,q)\longmapsto(a(q)Z,b(q)V,q).     \tag{OC.7}
148: \]
149: Evaluate the map and its inverse on the extending section
150: \((Z,V)=(1,1)\) of this chart. Both images remain in the open
151: central torus, so \(a,b\) and their inverses extend as
152: holomorphic units. Conversely (OC.7) extends on every chart of
153: the same fan. This proves the bounded map, not just an integral
154: valuation test.
155: 
156: In particular a meromorphic translation with valuation vector
157: \((j,v)\in\mathbb Z^2\) is reindexed by precisely
158: \[
159:        (j,v)=(3v-j)h+(2v-j)B.                             \tag{OC.8}
160: \]
161: After this period map its remaining coefficients are the units in
162: (OC.7). This identity is the mixed-cusp replacement for K41's
163: whole-zero calculation. Since the toric fan is periodic under the
164: actual lattice (OC.6), it holds for every transported index-one fan,
165: including the non-semifree choices. Its unit maps carry all Cox
166: characters and the physical delta action.
167: 
168: On the source I2, remove source-period powers from the first
169: coordinate in (OC.5). A source coordinate \(q^j a(q)\) is
170: then \(q^\epsilon a'(q)\), \(\epsilon=0\) or \(1\),
171: by division by the literal \(q^2H(q)\) period. These are the
172: smooth points on the two actual Neron components, respectively.
173: This gives the source extension of an endpoint through I2, with
174: its component retained. Its line multiplier still records the
175: second coordinate in (OC.8); the line order of \(h\) is one
176: and cannot be omitted.
177: 
178: ## OC.3. The global rational endpoint and its exact divisor
179: 
180: On the complement of the selected I2 the local line quotient is an
181: unramified regular covering by the actual line complement. To lift
182: a global extending translation, choose a nonzero vector along the
183: source zero section on each simply connected root or retained disc
184: and lift its image through that covering. The source point is a
185: holomorphic section meeting the regular locus of the reduced or
186: good fibre. Its line translation extends by the Hom evaluation
187: argument in OC.1. Matching its value at the chosen zero vector
188: makes its quotient exactly the given translation on every generic
189: compact torus, and hence on the full chart.
190: 
191: The lift commutes with \(B_\lambda\) by OC.1. Its conjugate
192: by the finite cap root differs by a deck power \(B_\lambda^a\).
193: Iterating the order \(m\) and using \(A^m=B^n\) gives
194: \(B^{ma}=1\); strict contraction forces \(a=0\). Thus the
195: lift retains the actual finite character. Overlap lifts differ by
196: integer suspension powers. The base complement is the affine line
197: with one finite orbifold point; its orbifold fundamental group is
198: cyclic of order \(m\), so it has no homomorphism to \(\mathbb Z\).
199: These powers can be patched to one lift. OC.2 gives its source
200: extension at the missing I2. The resulting original source section
201: is rational, and the proved Mordell--Weil group \(\mathbb ZG\)
202: makes it \(jG\) for an integer \(j\). The lifted line map is
203: therefore
204: \[
205:                             f(t)U^j                       \tag{OC.9}
206: \]
207: for a rational base function \(f\): its ratio with \(U^j\)
208: is constant on each compact generic elliptic fibre, and the root
209: and selected-cusp maps prove its meromorphic extension.
210: 
211: Retain K37's literal rational norm ratio
212: \[
213:                    \rho=B_0^{-1}/U^3.                    \tag{OC.10}
214: \]
215: At the I2 cusp the graph defects of \(U^3\) are
216: \(-2C_0-C_1\), whereas those of \(B_0^{-1}\) are
217: \(-C_0\). Thus their ratio has the whole-fibre divisor
218: \(+C_0+C_1\), and
219: \[
220:                   \operatorname{ord}_c\rho=1-3e_c.       \tag{OC.11}
221: \]
222: At every other site \(i\), write the actual line comparison
223: \(U=z_i^{e_i}u_i L_i\), with \(L_i\) invertible and
224: \(m_i=m\) at the good root, \(m_i=1\) elsewhere. The
225: source/line norm diagram gives
226: \[
227:  \operatorname{ord}_i\rho=-3e_i/m_i,\qquad
228:              e_c+\sum_{i\ne c}e_i/m_i=\frac13.           \tag{OC.12}
229: \]
230: The second equality is the degree of the actual rational divisor
231: of \(\rho\). At the good root its finite character is still
232: \(DLD^{-1}=\zeta_3L\), so \(e_i\equiv m/3\pmod m\);
233: the order in (OC.12) is integral. This retains the finite character,
234: not just its rational average.
235: 
236: For a fixed scalar, invertibility of (OC.9) away from the cusp
237: gives \(\operatorname{ord}_i f=-je_i/m_i\), including the
238: integrality at the root. Write
239: \(v=\operatorname{ord}_c f+je_c\) for its actual cusp line
240: valuation. Degree zero of \(\operatorname{div}f\) and
241: (OC.12) give
242: \[
243:                               v=j/3.                     \tag{OC.13}
244: \]
245: Both \(j\) and \(v\) are integers, so \(j=3a\).
246: Equation (OC.8) now has \(3v-j=0\), \(2v-j=-a\);
247: the exact cusp reindexing is \(B^{-a}\). Moreover \(f\)
248: and \(\rho^a\) have the same divisor at every site by
249: (OC.11)--(OC.13), so \(f=c_0\rho^a\) for a nonzero
250: constant. Therefore the global endpoint is literally
251: \[
252:                        fU^{3a}=c_0B_0^{-a}.              \tag{OC.14}
253: \]
254: On the quotient by \(B_\lambda=\lambda B_0\) this is
255: physical multiplication by \(c_0\lambda^a\). Every physical
256: nonzero scalar extends on every actual root and toric chart.
257: It acts faithfully because a nonzero suspension power has nonzero
258: source translation in the torsion-free Mordell--Weil group. Thus
259: the global extending translation group is exactly \(\mathbb C^*\).
260: This proof has tested the central order-three kernel and every finite
261: line character against the actual component cusp.
262: 
263: ## OC.4. Scalar winding, its root square, and the shorter endpoint
264: 
265: For the varying scalar use the universal parameter
266: \(v_\lambda=\log\lambda/(2\pi i m)\). A scalar winding
267: \(\ell\) changes it by \(\ell/m\). The actual OS49
268: ordinary-to-cap map is K37's plus-log map, with period
269: \(n(e_\gamma-e_u)+d\delta\). The source-period and fixed
270: delta terms have no scalar change. Consequently its exact change
271: on the root is
272: \[
273:                   \ell n\frac{\log z}{2\pi i}\delta.    \tag{OC.15}
274: \]
275: Let \(E\) be a forward endpoint from the initial model to the
276: continued final model, with ordinary expression \(fU^j\).
277: The lifting proof in OC.3 applies with the exact relation
278: \(A_{\rm final}E=EA_{\rm initial}\) in place of commutation:
279: a possible difference \(B^a\) has \(m\)-fold iterate
280: \(B^{ma}\), while both root norms are \(B^n\). It is
281: therefore zero by contraction. The two canonical root source maps
282: are identical; only their scalar factors differ. Their source
283: section and rational-base ratio are consequently those of OC.9.
284: The actual square is
285: \[
286:           \Phi_{\rm final}E=E_{\rm cap}\Phi_{\rm initial}.
287:                                                                \tag{OC.16}
288: \]
289: Thus the cap expression contains the extra factor \(z^{\ell n}\).
290: Its extending unit condition is exactly
291: \[
292:         m\operatorname{ord}_{i}f+je_i=-\ell n.            \tag{OC.17}
293: \]
294: At all other noncusp sites the condition is the fixed-scalar one.
295: The source and line characters in this square are the same as K37
296: OS.10--11, with the arrow reversed there because OS.9 is a closing
297: map. Taking degrees as before now gives
298: \[
299:              v=\frac j3+\frac{\ell n}{m},\qquad
300:                       3v-j=\frac{\ell n}{k}.             \tag{OC.18}
301: \]
302: The left side is an integer and \(n=\pm1\), so a closed
303: marked family necessarily has
304: \[
305:                               \ell=Nk,\quad N\in\mathbb Z.
306:                                                                \tag{OC.19}
307: \]
308: This is an obstruction from the actual mixed-cusp lattice and the
309: root square, not divisibility of an already computed sphere class.
310: 
311: P3K.37 supplies the actual endpoint for every such winding. For one
312: positive shorter turn its closing map is
313: \(\widetilde C_r:X_{\zeta_3r}\to X_r\) with source
314: translation \(-2nG\). Its inverse is the forward endpoint with
315: \(j=2n\). Equation (OC.18) then gives \(v=n\), and its
316: valuation vector is
317: \[
318:                            (2n,n)=n h.                  \tag{OC.20}
319: \]
320: This is exactly the inverse of K37's bounded closing reindexing.
321: At the root, \(j\equiv-n\pmod3\) gives the full character
322: equation with \(DLD^{-1}=\zeta_3L\) and
323: \(A_{\zeta_3r}=\zeta_3^n A_r\); the fixed \(d\)
324: commutes and is retained. K37's rational cube root, actual norm,
325: constant delta adjustment, and common Cox origin therefore give a
326: literal closed endpoint. Iterating it \(N\) times, or using its
327: inverse for negative \(N\), realizes (OC.19) with the same fan.
328: 
329: ## OC.5. The actual interval comparison and physical return
330: 
331: OC.1's parameter-dependent source and line maps and the full
332: groupoid Lie solution give an actual interval comparison to that
333: canonical scalar family. The cusp maps are precisely OC.2's bounded
334: units after its integer period reindexings. They preserve the fan
335: and transport its Cox normal maps, so no extra framing is inserted.
336: The ratio of any given endpoint of winding (OC.19) to K37's
337: constructed endpoint is a global extending translation of the same
338: family. OC.3 identifies it with a physical scalar, including every
339: finite kernel. Its chosen logarithm gives a global scalar isotopy.
340: 
341: Write the actual interval comparison as \(I_t:X_t\to Y_t\),
342: the forward endpoints as \(E_X,E_Y\), and the reference parallel
343: transport as \(P_Y\). Then for a physical scalar \(C\),
344: \[
345:  I_1E_XI_0^{-1}=C E_Y,\qquad
346:  I_0(E_X^{-1}I_1^{-1}P_YI_0)I_0^{-1}
347:                   =E_Y^{-1}C^{-1}P_Y.                   \tag{OC.21}
348: \]
349: Removing \(C\) by the actual scalar isotopy leaves the inverse
350: reference endpoint followed by parallel transport. This is exactly
351: the physical return of K37, iterated \(N\) times. In particular
352: fixed scalar gives the trivial sphere bundle, and for a transported
353: fan with \(S=a^2-ab+b^2\) the oriented return is
354: \[
355:                     N\frac{k}{4}(S-1)(S-3)\pmod{28}.     \tag{OC.22}
356: \]
357: The formula uses the constructed shorter family \(\lambda=w^k\)
358: and its actual endpoint, not a formal third of a return class.
